 "verdict": "THE LOCAL-INFLOW ASSOCIATION SURVIVES THE SITE RANDOM EFFECT, AND THE OUTCOME'S VARIANCE IS DOMINATED BY THE WORLD RATHER THAN BY REPEATS OF A WORLD. Over the 311 runs of 11 mixed cells (135 fused, share 0.4341), collapsed into 258 worlds (1.205 runs per world) and 143 phase classes, the logistic slope of fusion on the standardised local inflow at the colder body is 2.477 per sd under a random intercept per world (profile 95 % [1.1273, 3.0925], cluster bootstrap [1.2343, 4.2702]; likelihood ratio against a zero slope 19.02 on 1 df, p = 1.29e-05). The between-world standard deviation is sigma = 8.37 (mode of its own profile 6.32; 95 % profile interval [3.7547, 7.8318], cluster bootstrap [7.9051, 8.6200]) and the ICC is 0.955 at the joint fit and 0.924 at the profile mode (interval [0.8108, 0.9491]): essentially all of the latent-scale variance is between worlds, not between runs of a world. That is the falsifier of the article's opening claim refuted in its own terms: the dispersion does NOT reduce to between-family noise that the site random effect absorbs, because the fixed effect of the local inflow remains non-zero and in the same direction as the fixed-effects reading (0.590 at sigma = 0), with the conditional slope the larger of the two (ratio 4.20), as a logistic mixed model requires. With a random intercept per phase class the slope is 0.983 (95 % [0.5893, 1.5270], sigma = 2.52, ICC 0.660), so the result does not depend on which of the two nested site definitions is used. The midpoint covariate keeps its published negative sign in both variants (-8.045 world, -1.547 phase). Criteria: 5 of 5 passed (C1_association_survives_world, C2_site_carries_the_dispersion, C3_no_material_attenuation, C4_phase_class_variant, C5_published_midpoint_reproduced). LIMITATIONS, ALL REGISTERED IN ADVANCE: (1) the local inflow is a deterministic function of (cell, phase), so it is exactly constant within every multi-run world (0 of 47 worlds vary at all) and this model identifies its slope BETWEEN worlds only - there is no within-world slope in this design to estimate; (2) sigma is interior but weakly identified, its 95 % profile interval spanning [3.7547, 7.8318] in sigma units (0.8108 to 0.9491 in ICC), with the slope co-moving along that ridge, so the pair (slope, sigma) is pinned jointly and not separately - the sign and the non-zero-ness of the slope are robust, its magnitude is not; (3) the estimator registered in the plan was a Laplace approximation, which is not adequate here: at the fitted parameters it differs from the adaptive Gauss-Hermite quadrature by -2.33 log-likelihood units already at sigma = 2 (1.439 against 2.477 for the slope), so the reported fit is the quadrature one and the Laplace fit is carried beside it for the record; (4) the profile intervals are interpolated on a (slope, sigma) grid, and the ridge is flat enough that the joint maximum sits a fraction of a grid step outside the interval centred on the profile mode - the grid maximum falls only 2.203 log-likelihood units below the joint fit, which bounds how far the interval ends can be off - and the aggregate records the point-in-interval checks explicitly rather than hiding them."
}
